<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amenhotep III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1390–1352 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amenhotep III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> in the fourteenth century BCE. His long reign was a period of peace, wealth and monumental building, and he conducted extensive diplomacy with other great powers. He represents the height of the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>'s prosperity, and he was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii.jpg" alt="Amenhotep III" width="299" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by A. Parrot; sculptor unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colossal_Amenhotep_III_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amenhotep III was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a> and a minor wife, Mutemwiya, and was born about 1401 BCE. He probably became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> at between 6 and 12 years of age. A regent would likely have ruled until he came of age, but none is attested in the surviving records. In his second regnal year he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>, daughter of Yuya and Thuya, who remained Great Royal Wife throughout his reign. They had at least two sons, Thutmose and the future Amenhotep IV, and at least four daughters. He also married daughters of the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Arzawa, and, in his last decade, at least two of his own daughters, Sitamun and Iset.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The only military campaign in which he is known to have taken part was against a rebellion in Kush in his fifth year, commemorated on stelae near Aswan and Saï. His court is known mainly from officials' tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, among them the viziers Ramose and Ptahmose and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, Merimose. He built the palace of Malkata, begun about his eleventh year, and moved there permanently around year 29. He held three Sed festivals, in years 30, 34 and 37, jubilees that affirmed the king's fitness to rule and his transition to divinity.</p><p>Diplomatic letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> and other states survive among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna Letters</a>, often asking for gold. In one, the Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadashman-enlil-i">Kadashman-Enlil I</a> quotes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> refusing him an Egyptian princess: no daughter of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is given to anyone. Amenhotep did take foreign princesses as wives. In his thirty-sixth year the Mitannian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> sent the statue of Ishtar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> to Egypt, most likely to bless the pharaoh's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadukhipa">Tadukhipa</a>. Historians disagree whether he shared a coregency with Amenhotep IV; most now argue for none, or at most two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His latest attested date is year 38, and he may have died early in year 39. Reliefs show him weak, and scientists believe he suffered from arthritis and obesity, with painful, worn teeth. He was buried in tomb WV22 in the Western Valley. His mummy was later moved to the cache in KV35, where Victor Loret found it in 1898. He left more surviving statues than any other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, over 250, and built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. His mortuary temple fell into ruin within two hundred years, leaving the Colossi of Memnon, each 18 m high. His son Amenhotep IV, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, succeeded him and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amenhotep III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Amarna letters of the fourteenth century BCE: King Rib-Hadda wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs <b>Amenhotep III</b> and Akhenaten, pleading for archers and supplies as Abdi-Ashirta of Amurru and then his son Aziru seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thutmose III would prove the most consequential military pharaoh in Egyptian history, and the golden age of <b>Amenhotep III</b> rested on the territory that he conquered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom sealed its alliance with Egypt through royal marriages: a daughter of Artatama I married Thutmose IV, and Shuttarna II's daughter Gilukhipa and Tushratta's daughter Tadukhipa were sent to <b>Amenhotep III</b>, which reinforced cultural exchange and confirmed Mitanni's great-power status.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna letters, found in Egypt in 1887, contain the correspondence of the pharaohs <b>Amenhotep III</b> and Akhenaten with the kings of Babylon, Assyria, Mitanni and the Hittites and with the rulers of Canaan, almost all in Akkadian.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>A list of foreign places on a statue base from the mortuary temple of <b>Amenhotep III</b> at Thebes in Egypt, of the early 14th century BCE, names places in Crete and probably Mycenae on the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 14th century BCE Arzawa was strong enough for <b>Amenhotep III</b> of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom to negotiate a marriage with the daughter of King Tarhundaradu; the two Amarna letters involved are written in Hittite.</blockquote><a href="/state/arzawa">Arzawa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Malkata, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=-1371" aria-label="Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5313 1173 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amenhotep III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5763 1443h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5763 1443h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=-1371">What was here in 1371 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
